WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.915
Welcome to Module 4, Setting up R and
GGIR. In this lesson, we

00:00:04.915 --> 00:00:09.829
will turn raw GT3X accelerometer files
into a GGIR run that is

00:00:09.829 --> 00:00:13.000
organized, verifiable, and reproducible.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.851
You will build the project structure,
validate the inputs, run

00:00:16.851 --> 00:00:20.516
the first five GGIR parts, inspect the
outputs, and archive

00:00:20.516 --> 00:00:23.000
the evidence needed for quality control.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.404
By the end of this module, you will be
able to organize a GGIR

00:00:27.404 --> 00:00:30.753
project, verify the raw inputs, run the
core GGIR pipeline in

00:00:30.753 --> 00:00:34.157
Parts 1 through 5, check the resulting
milestones and reports,

00:00:34.157 --> 00:00:37.122
and archive the records needed for quality
control and

00:00:37.122 --> 00:00:38.000
reproducibility.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.941
Set up and document R, RStudio, and GGIR.
Install GGIR from

00:00:45.941 --> 00:00:51.000
CRAN with a single install.packages()
call.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.639
Notice that GGIR runs as one documented
function call, so

00:00:54.639 --> 00:00:57.000
there is no need for the older, separate
functions.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.086
Just as important as installing the
software is recording the

00:01:01.086 --> 00:01:04.222
environment around it. The R and GGIR
versions, the time zone,

00:01:04.222 --> 00:01:07.257
the device type and placement, the input
and output folders,

00:01:07.257 --> 00:01:09.988
the operator, the date of processing, and
the approved

00:01:09.988 --> 00:01:11.000
protocol parameters.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.526
Capturing these nine details up front is
what makes every

00:01:15.526 --> 00:01:18.000
later result reproducible and auditable.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.471
Create a new RStudio project for the
analysis. Open the new

00:01:22.471 --> 00:01:25.941
project wizard, choose new directory, then
new project, and

00:01:25.941 --> 00:01:29.529
give it a generic name inside a dedicated
course folder where

00:01:29.529 --> 00:01:31.000
it is easy to find later.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.288
A project gives every relative path the
same starting point,

00:01:35.288 --> 00:01:38.356
so the script, raw inputs, GGIR outputs,
quality control

00:01:38.356 --> 00:01:40.000
files, and logs stay together.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.976
When the project opens, confirm its name
in the RStudio title

00:01:43.976 --> 00:01:47.000
bar, and check that the files pane shows
the new project file.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.211
With the workspace anchored this way, the
console is ready to

00:01:51.211 --> 00:01:54.368
be prepared for a clean processing run.
The next step resets

00:01:54.368 --> 00:01:55.000
the console.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.353
Start with a clean R session, remove
existing objects, run

00:02:01.353 --> 00:02:04.936
garbage collection, and set consistent
options before creating

00:02:04.936 --> 00:02:08.000
any project folders. This keeps the run
reproducible.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.441
Create separate folders for raw input,
GGIR output, quality

00:02:14.441 --> 00:02:17.000
control review, logs, and scripts.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.475
Numbered prefixes keep them sorted in
pipeline order, and one

00:02:21.475 --> 00:02:25.006
dir.create() call builds the full set at
once. A folder status

00:02:25.006 --> 00:02:27.000
table confirms every folder exists.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.571
Then, open a new R script so every command
in this lesson is

00:02:30.571 --> 00:02:31.000
preserved.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.252
Install GGIR from CRAN if it is not
already available, then

00:02:35.252 --> 00:02:38.449
load the package and capture the version
that was actually

00:02:38.449 --> 00:02:39.000
installed.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.211
Next, pin the expected GGIR version and
stop immediately if

00:02:43.211 --> 00:02:46.367
the installed version does not match, so
every run of this

00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:48.000
course uses the same software.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.489
Record the session details in an
environment record, the R

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:55.977
version, GGIR version, study time zone,
operator code, and

00:02:55.977 --> 00:02:57.000
protocol version.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.182
Finally, add two small helper functions
that refuse to write

00:03:01.182 --> 00:03:04.364
empty records, protecting the archive
files created later in

00:03:04.364 --> 00:03:05.000
this lesson.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.067
Set the study time zone and create a
unique run ID. Use it to

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:18.133
separate GGIR output, quality control
review, and log folders

00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:19.000
for this run.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.326
Validate the input folder before running
GGIR. List every GT3X

00:03:24.326 --> 00:03:28.023
file and stop the run if the folder is
missing, if no

00:03:28.023 --> 00:03:32.000
recordings are found, or if unexpected
file types appear.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.085
Then confirm the file names contain no
spaces and that the

00:03:36.085 --> 00:03:38.000
input and output directories differ.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.355
Create two input manifests. Each row lists
a file with its

00:03:43.355 --> 00:03:46.595
size in megabytes so the whole batch can
be checked at a

00:03:46.595 --> 00:03:47.000
glance.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.013
The private manifest preserves the
original file names for

00:03:51.013 --> 00:03:54.234
internal traceability while the public
manifest replaces every

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.000
name with an anonymous input code.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.087
Both are written into the run log as
separate CSV files, so

00:04:00.087 --> 00:04:03.174
the version shared in this lesson shows
only the anonymized

00:04:03.174 --> 00:04:06.000
table. Both records stay together for the
audit trail.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.952
GGIR is organized as a sequence of
processing parts. Setting

00:04:10.952 --> 00:04:14.904
mode to 1 through 5 runs the complete core
pipeline and each

00:04:14.904 --> 00:04:18.000
part builds on milestone files created
earlier.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.634
This is why we validate the inputs first
and check the

00:04:21.634 --> 00:04:23.000
milestones after processing.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.770
Here is the whole analysis in one place. A
single, documented

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:31.169
GGIR call. Everything runs through this
one user-facing

00:04:31.169 --> 00:04:35.000
function. There are no legacy
sub-functions to chain together.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.265
Writing one argument per line keeps the
script easy to read,

00:04:39.265 --> 00:04:42.639
easy to diff, and easy to audit,
especially when it lives in a

00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:44.000
dated file like this one.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.810
One caution before we go further. The
values shown here are

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:50.000
examples for teaching, not universal
defaults.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.144
Every argument is a study decision, so set
each one from your

00:04:54.144 --> 00:04:56.000
own protocol before running a batch.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.591
Group the arguments by what they actually
control. Folders:

00:05:00.591 --> 00:05:04.000
datadir and outputdir tell GGIR where to
read and write.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.667
mode sets which numbered parts run, and
do.enmo requests ENMO

00:05:08.667 --> 00:05:12.273
calculation as the acceleration metric.
The recording-window

00:05:12.273 --> 00:05:15.699
arguments handle day masking and how much
of each file is

00:05:15.699 --> 00:05:16.000
kept.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:19.714
Sleep detection sets the wrist angle rules
that find each

00:05:19.714 --> 00:05:20.000
night.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.155
Intensity cut points, split light,
moderate and vigorous

00:05:24.155 --> 00:05:27.535
activity, and the output windows choose
Midnight to Midnight

00:05:27.535 --> 00:05:29.000
or Wake to Wake summaries.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.292
For every one of these, document the study
decision behind the

00:05:33.292 --> 00:05:36.000
value. The numbers here are examples, not
defaults.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.847
Review the GGIR call before running it.
Modes 1 through 5

00:05:40.847 --> 00:05:44.693
process the core pipeline, and f0 = 1, f1
= 0 selects all

00:05:44.693 --> 00:05:48.000
input files. The finished call prints for
review.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.957
Run the complete GGIR call. The console
shows part 1 starting,

00:05:52.957 --> 00:05:56.915
while GGIR processes the accelerometer
files with the settings

00:05:56.915 --> 00:05:58.000
we just reviewed.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:01.913
Parts 1 through 5 then run in sequence,
from calibration all

00:06:01.913 --> 00:06:04.000
the way through the person-level
summaries.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:09.000
The GGIR call returns successfully after 7
and a half minutes.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.797
Locate the study output and record the run
summary using

00:06:13.797 --> 00:06:18.000
project-relative paths. The completion
table confirms success.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:23.306
A completed analysis also needs a
traceable run record. We

00:06:23.306 --> 00:06:26.839
will preserve the run identifier, software
version, time zone,

00:06:26.839 --> 00:06:29.974
configuration, manifest, warnings,
checksums, and exact

00:06:29.974 --> 00:06:31.000
processing script.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.690
Public records remain anonymized, while
private records retain

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.000
internal traceability.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.919
Now test the core configuration, metadata,
milestones, and

00:06:42.919 --> 00:06:44.000
results folders.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.446
All completion checks pass, confirming
that the core run

00:06:48.446 --> 00:06:49.000
finished.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.246
Create private and anonymized public
inventories for all

00:06:53.246 --> 00:06:54.000
output files.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.000
Count milestones, tables, plots, and PDF
reports.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.653
Confirm that the saved script is
non-empty, then generate its

00:07:04.653 --> 00:07:09.000
MD5 checksum. The checksum fingerprints
the exact script.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:13.615
Archive the run metadata, software
session, package citation,

00:07:13.615 --> 00:07:16.933
warnings, errors, sanitized configuration,
and the exact

00:07:16.933 --> 00:07:18.000
processing script.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.966
Before archiving, the configuration is
redacted so that the

00:07:21.966 --> 00:07:24.631
real input and output paths are replaced
with generic

00:07:24.631 --> 00:07:27.648
placeholders, keeping private folder names
out of the shared

00:07:27.648 --> 00:07:28.000
record.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.609
Together, these records explain what was
run, which software

00:07:31.609 --> 00:07:34.000
versions were used, and whether any issues
need review.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.873
Preserving them alongside the output makes
the analysis

00:07:37.873 --> 00:07:41.112
auditable and fully reproducible without
ever exposing private

00:07:41.112 --> 00:07:42.000
file identifiers.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.000
Create private and public quality control
manifests.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.582
Anonymous QC codes are ready, with every
item marked pending

00:07:52.582 --> 00:07:53.000
review.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:57.000
Run the final archive validation.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:01.396
Every required record must exist and be
non-empty, including

00:08:01.396 --> 00:08:04.623
the processing script, run metadata,
session information,

00:08:04.623 --> 00:08:07.906
package citation, file manifests,
sanitized configuration,

00:08:07.906 --> 00:08:11.075
warnings, errors, completion checks,
inventories, output

00:08:11.075 --> 00:08:13.000
counts, and quality control files.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.620
The validation walks the whole list and
flags anything missing

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:17.000
or empty.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.268
Only when every check returns true does
the script update the

00:08:21.268 --> 00:08:24.000
run status to success and save the archive
summary.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.946
At that point, the Module 4 workflow is
complete, fully

00:08:27.946 --> 00:08:31.000
documented, and ready for the quality
review in Module 5.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.392
You have now created a structured project,
processed every

00:08:35.392 --> 00:08:38.842
input through GGIR parts 1 to 5, verified
the core outputs,

00:08:38.842 --> 00:08:42.000
and archived the evidence required for
quality review.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.650
The workflow is complete, reproducible,
and ready for the next

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:48.000
stage of analysis.
